      "latency_ms": 2539.0,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 656,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen** and **oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**—meaning every single molecule of water is made of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, to humans, Earth, and all known life, water is much more than just a chemical formula. Here is a breakdown of what water is, why it matters, and how it behaves:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Physical States of Water\nWater is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states:\n*   **Liquid:** What we typically drink, swim in, and rain from the sky.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water, found in glaciers, snow, ice cubes, and hail.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Water in the air as humidity, invisible vapor, or clouds.\n\n### 2. Unique and Miraculous Properties\nWater has several bizarre and unique scientific properties that make life possible:\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. Because of this, it acts as a delivery system, carrying vital chemicals, minerals, and nutrients through our bodies and the ecosystem.\n*   **Ice Floats:** Almost all substances shrink and get denser when they freeze. Water is different; when it freezes into ice, it expands and becomes *less* dense. This is why ice floats on top of lakes, allowing fish and marine life to survive underneath during the winter.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This is why oceans help regulate the climate of the entire planet, and why our bodies don't overheat instantly.\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some bugs to walk on water) and to other things (adhesion—which is how water travels up the roots of giant trees).\n\n### 3. Water and Life\n*   **Biological Necessity:** No known form of life can survive without water. The human body is made up of about 60% water. It regulates our temperature, lubricates joints, flushes out waste, and protects our organs.\n*   **The Cradle of Evolution:** Scientists believe life on Earth originally began in the oceans billions of years ago.\n\n### 4. Water on Earth\n*   **The Blue Planet:** About 71% of the Earth's surface is covered in water, mostly by oceans.\n*   **Scarcity:** Despite how vast the oceans look, **about 96.5% of Earth's water is saltwater** (which humans cannot drink or use for crops). Of the remaining 3% that is freshwater, most is locked away in glaciers and polar ice caps, leaving less than 1% easily accessible for human use in lakes, rivers, and underground aquifers. \n\nIn short, water is the lifeblood of our planet—a simple molecule that creates, sustains, and shapes the world around us."
